WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.741
A rebuttal is more than a second constructive.

00:00:02.741 --> 00:00:05.482
First identify the argument you are answering.

00:00:05.482 --> 00:00:08.342
Restate it fairly, then locate the disagreement.

00:00:08.342 --> 00:00:15.135
You might contest the evidence, the causal link, the size of an impact, or the
comparison between competing harms.

00:00:16.335 --> 00:00:19.466
Use a simple pattern: signpost, answer, explain.

00:00:19.466 --> 00:00:23.379
On their first contention, identify the mechanism they need.

00:00:23.379 --> 00:00:25.336
Give a reason it may not hold.

00:00:25.336 --> 00:00:28.010
Then explain why that reduces the impact.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:33.880
A label such as no link is not a complete answer until you explain the missing
connection.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.564
Two teams can both identify real harms.

00:00:37.564 --> 00:00:39.794
The judge still needs a comparison.

00:00:39.794 --> 00:00:43.744
Consider magnitude, probability, timeframe, and reversibility.

00:00:43.744 --> 00:00:47.566
Explain why the dimension you choose matters in this debate.

00:00:47.566 --> 00:00:49.541
Do not merely list those words.

00:00:49.541 --> 00:00:52.790
Use them to resolve the specific clash on the flow.
